ones and outside spending on his behalf. Most professional raters call the race Likely or Solid Republican, and prediction markets give Jones about a one-in-six chance of winning.\n\nStill, the only public polls of the race, a nonpartisan Hendrix College survey from August and a Democratic-sponsored poll from May, both showed Jones narrowly ahead, and the national environment favors Democrats this year. Jones has drawn national attention, endorsements from Harris and surrogates like Joe Kennedy III, and a town-hall swing through the district, though Republicans have attacked him over a missing financial disclosure. Hill also touts federal grants for the Port of Little Rock. The race looks like a Lean Republican contest that a strong Democratic wave could make genuinely competitive.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1835, "margin": -5.2}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.3532, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.3233, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2423, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2933, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.1983, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.3205, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.3445, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.2683, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.2665, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.2648, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.2693, "margin": -4.5}]}